        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "2d1d689b-5e59-5f58-9089-32076e85cc06",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Mary Wollstonecraft\n- Works: A Vindication of the Rights of Woman\n- Topics: feminist philosophy, women's rights, women's education\n\nFacts:\n- Mary Wollstonecraft was born on 27 April 1759 and died on 10 September 1797.\n- Mary Wollstonecraft was an English writer, philosopher, and advocate of women's rights.\n- Mary Wollstonecraft wrote A Vindication of the Rights of Woman: with Strictures on Political and Moral Subjects in 1792.\n- A Vindication of the Rights of Woman is one of the earliest works of feminist philosophy.\n- In A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, Mary Wollstonecraft argued that women should receive an education appropriate to their position in society.\n- Mary Wollstonecraft claimed that women are essential to the nation because they educate its children and can be companions to their husbands rather than merely wives.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d932e3b9-bdd1-5f98-9af0-074cfcf625ab",
        "text": "Page: A Vindication of the Rights of Woman. Sentence: A Vindication of the Rights of Woman: with Strictures on Political and Moral Subjects (1792), written by the 18th-century British proto-feminist Mary Wollstonecraft, is one of the earliest works of feminist philosophy.\nPage: A Vindication of the Rights of Woman. Sentence: She argues that women ought to have an education commensurate with their position in society, claiming that women are essential to the nation because they educate its children and because they could be \"companions\" to their husbands, rather than mere wives.\nPage: Mary Wollstonecraft. Sentence: Mary Wollstonecraft ( ; 27 April 1759 – 10 September 1797) was an English writer, philosopher, and advocate of women's rights.",
